@*
    AES-256-GCM through Web Crypto, with the key derived from a passphrase by PBKDF2.

    GCM authenticates as well as encrypts, so an altered message fails to decrypt rather than
    returning wrong text. The salt and nonce are random for every message and are carried in
    front of the ciphertext, which is why the same text encrypted twice never looks the same.
*@

<div class="tool" data-tool="encrypt">
    <div class="tool-field">
        <label for="encrypt-in">Message</label>
        <textarea id="encrypt-in" data-tool-input rows="6" spellcheck="false"
                  placeholder="Text to encrypt, or a message from this tool to decrypt"></textarea>
    </div>

    <div class="tool-field">
        <label for="encrypt-key">Passphrase</label>
        <input type="text" id="encrypt-key" data-tool-option="password" spellcheck="false"
               autocomplete="off" placeholder="The passphrase both sides share" />
        <span class="tool-hint">
            The only thing protecting the message. There is no way to recover the text
            without it.
        </span>
    </div>

    <div class="tool-field">
        <label for="encrypt-out">Result</label>
        <textarea id="encrypt-out" data-tool-output rows="6" spellcheck="false" readonly></textarea>
    </div>

    <div class="tool-actions">
        <button type="button" class="btn" data-tool-run="encrypt">Encrypt</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn" data-tool-run="decrypt">Decrypt</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" data-tool-copy>Copy result</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" data-tool-swap>Result to message</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" data-tool-clear>Clear</button>
    </div>

    <p class="tool-status" data-tool-status role="status"></p>

    <p class="tool-hint">
        The output is Base64 over a 16 byte salt, a 12 byte nonce and the ciphertext with its
        authentication tag. The key is derived with PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256 over 600,000 rounds,
        which is what makes a short passphrase expensive to guess and why encrypting takes a
        moment. Only this tool reads the format.
    </p>
</div>
